        },
        "ch_doubt": {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "01 · The doubt",
            "heading": "A £30-a-month habit built on a mechanism that doesn't exist",
            "text": "<p>You have been taking it every morning. Eight months, maybe longer. Maybe your nails are better. Maybe that is the water you started drinking at the same time. You keep buying it because stopping feels like undoing something, even though you could not say what.</p><p>That uncertainty is not you failing to pay attention. It is the entire commercial proposition. Collagen is one of the fastest-growing categories in supplements, and it is built on an assumption nobody selling it has to defend: that swallowing a protein sends it to the place you want it. The pictures on the tub do the arguing. The label never has to.</p><p>It is a good business. Cheap to make, easy to explain, impossible to disprove from your own bathroom - and a product you cannot evaluate is a product you never cancel. Here is what is actually happening to it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "ch_mechanism": {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "02 · The mechanism",
            "heading": "Where it actually goes",
            "text": "<p>Collagen is a protein, and your digestive system treats it like any other. Enzymes break it down into individual amino acids and short peptides. Those enter the bloodstream as a general supply, mixed in with everything else you have eaten.</p><p>There is no courier service. Nothing tags those amino acids as skin-bound. Your body allocates them the way it allocates all protein - according to what it is currently doing, in an order it decides.</p>"
          }
        },
        "step1": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "01",
            "title": "Broken apart",
            "text": "<p>Digestive enzymes reduce the scoop to amino acids. The collagen no longer exists as collagen.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "step2": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "02",
            "title": "Pooled, not posted",
            "text": "<p>They join one general supply. Your body spends it wherever it is already working.</p>",
            "dark": false
          }
        },
        "step3": {
          "type": "step",
          "settings": {
            "numeral": "03",
            "title": "Burned or stored",
            "text": "<p>Surplus is not kept as protein. Nitrogen is excreted and the carbon left behind becomes glucose or fat.</p>",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "ch_fuel": {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "",
            "heading": "",
            "text": "<p>Published metabolic research is unambiguous on that last step. Protein you have no current use for is not banked for later - it is fuel, and expensive fuel at that.</p>"
          }
        },
        "callout": {
          "type": "callout",
          "settings": {
            "label": "The short version",
            "title": "Eating collagen does not put collagen in your skin, any more than eating steak builds biceps.",
            "text": "<p>Protein you don't have a use for is expensive fuel, nothing more.</p>",
            "figure": "",
            "dark": true
          }
        },
        "fig_cells": {
          "type": "figure",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/bioscience-image-library-by-fayette-reynolds-rVG2ADmYf8Q-unsplash_3.png",
            "alt": "Macro cell imagery, oil-and-water abstract",
            "height": 380
          }
        },
        "ch_built": {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "03 · The real constraint",
            "heading": "Nothing in your body is delivered. It is built.",
            "text": "<p>Collagen is not a special case. Every structure you have is manufactured on site by cells - skin, muscle, bone, hair, the lining of your gut, the enzymes that digest your food, the neurotransmitters that decide what kind of mood you are in. Cells run all of it.</p><p>And every one of those jobs costs energy. Which is why supplying more raw material rarely changes much: if amino acids were the bottleneck, anyone eating enough protein would already have solved this. The constraint is not what the cell has to work with. It is how much work the cell can afford to do.</p>"
          }
        },
        "ch_funds": {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "kicker": "04 · What funds it",
            "heading": "One molecule pays for all of it",
            "text": "<p>NAD⁺ is the coenzyme mitochondria use to make energy, and the same one repair enzymes need to clear damage. Every cell in your body depends on it - which means everything downstream of a cell does too.</p><p>It declines steadily with age. By your forties you are running on roughly half what you had at 25. Nothing feels broken. Output is simply lower everywhere at once, which is why the changes people notice in their thirties and forties arrive together rather than one at a time - energy, recovery, sleep, skin.</p>"
          }
        },
        "stat": {
          "type": "stat",
          "settings": {
            "value": "+51%",
            "caption": "<p>Blood NAD⁺ against participants' own baseline after 14 days of nicotinamide riboside supplementation.¹</p>"
          }
        },
